{
  "platform_id": "0Mss7hxdqvQ",
  "profile": {
    "video_id": "kenji:0Mss7hxdqvQ",
    "purpose": "Show step by step how to roast a whole chicken so the breast stays juicy while the legs cook through, using a stovetop sear before oven roasting, then how to carve it.",
    "video_type": "how_to",
    "thesis": "Searing the back and thighs of a dry-brined, trussed whole chicken in a hot skillet gives the legs a head start, so the legs and breast reach their target temperatures at the same time in a hot oven.",
    "key_takeaways": [
      "Breast meat is best around 150–155°F, while leg meat needs at least 165–180°F, so a whole bird needs uneven heat.",
      "Salt the chicken and rest it uncovered on a rack in the fridge overnight, but no more than about two nights, so the skin dries slightly and crisps rather than turning leathery.",
      "Poke the fat deposits all over with a skewer and cut small slits near the backbone so rendered fat can escape and the skin crisps.",
      "Kenji argues trussing actually slows leg cooking and is mainly for looks, but searing the legs cancels that effect.",
      "Sear the back and then each thigh side in a smoking-hot cast iron pan for about 5 minutes, until pale golden, then brush with rendered fat and roast at 425°F convection (or 450°F without convection).",
      "Pull the chicken at 150°F in the breast, check that the legs are 165°F or more, and rest it before carving.",
      "Basting during roasting is unnecessary; if you do baste, use pure fat rather than butter.",
      "Carve the legs off first, getting the oyster, then take the breasts off the bone; removing the wishbone before roasting makes carving easier."
    ],
    "outline": [
      {
        "title": "Introduction and preparation",
        "summary": "Kenji explains why breast and legs need different temperatures and introduces a hybrid method based on his Cook's Illustrated technique and Daniel Gritzer's Serious Eats recipe. He then shows the pre-salted chicken and pokes its fat deposits and cuts slits so fat can render out.",
        "first_segment": 0,
        "last_segment": 2
      },
      {
        "title": "Trussing and seasoning",
        "summary": "A simple trussing method using twine around the breast and legs, with the wing tips tucked, followed by stuffing the cavity with sage, rosemary and thyme.",
        "first_segment": 2,
        "last_segment": 5
      },
      {
        "title": "Searing the chicken",
        "summary": "The chicken is seared back-side down and then on each thigh in a hot cast iron skillet to give the legs a head start. Kenji discusses why trussing affects leg cooking speed and why presentation matters for flavor perception.",
        "first_segment": 5,
        "last_segment": 10
      },
      {
        "title": "Roasting in the oven",
        "summary": "An inserted note explains overnight uncovered dry-brining on a rack. Kenji then inserts a multi-probe thermometer, brushes the bird with rendered fat, explains why not to baste, and roasts at 425°F convection in a toaster oven.",
        "first_segment": 10,
        "last_segment": 13
      },
      {
        "title": "Resting and checking temp",
        "summary": "Kenji parboils Yukon Gold potatoes to roast in the chicken fat, pulls the chicken at 150°F, watches carryover cooking, and checks leg and breast temperatures before resting.",
        "first_segment": 13,
        "last_segment": 15
      },
      {
        "title": "Carving the chicken",
        "summary": "After resting to about 145°F, Kenji removes the twine, takes off the legs with the oysters, splits drumsticks and thighs, and removes the breasts from the bone, with tips on wishbone removal and crispy skin.",
        "first_segment": 16,
        "last_segment": 20
      },
      {
        "title": "Summary and conclusions",
        "summary": "A recap of the key steps and times, followed by a plug for his books and Book Larder.",
        "first_segment": 20,
        "last_segment": 23
      }
    ],
